bloodborne is an action role-playing  game made by Japanese developer from  software brought to you by the same  creative team that made Demon Souls and  Dark Souls the story is set in a  Lovecraft meets Cronenberg meets Dickens  meets Bram Stoker dreamscape that is a  fantastic example of the cosmic horror  genre and that's why we're going to talk  about how the game's happiest ending is  actually its most depressing  it's become conventionals to talk about  games with multiple endings in terms of  the good ending versus the bad ending or  the good ending versus the evil ending  and so on and so forth because most  games with multiple endings do tend to  have a fairly unambiguous hierarchy  usually with the optimal ending being  more difficult with additional steps  required or just a generally superior  grasp of the game as hold needed jumping  through various hoops to save every  secondary character results in an ending  where almost everyone lives happily ever  after the end  blood-borne however inverts this typical  relationship providing three endings  that on a surface level are happier the  less effort you put into the game  story-wise the game doesn't branch much  through the bulk of its playtime  approaching things in a different order  or making key decisions will change the  fate of various individuals but the  overall plot remains ultimately fixed it  is intentionally obtuse bordering on  inscrutable something that is 100%  appropriate for the cosmic horror genre  even when the reader has the big picture  they are still left with an EIN  sensation that this big picture is but a  postage stamp-sized piece of a much much  bigger picture that they are not and  never will be privy to so the plot of  blood-borne starts long before the game  begins and it intentionally paints only  a vague sketch of events that happened  over the course of hundreds of years  ages and ages ago there was a  civilization called the Thumb Aryans who  had a connection with the old ones  otherworldly beings of immense power who  they worshipped as gods queen yharnam  their last ruler had a particularly  powerful connection to these beings but  things started to go bad so that the  Marion's retreated into their catacombs  and sealed themselves away over time a  town named yharnam after the Queen was  founded on the ruins and explorers  eventually found their way into the  catacombs where they discovered dark  secrets these dark secrets led to the  foundation of the Academy of Bergen  worth built studied things found in the  labyrinth and the rise of a cult called  the choir who in turn used that  knowledge to develop something called  blood ministrations had by doping blood  with various kinds of alien fluids  referred to as the old blood the choir  was able to perform healing miracles  give people superpowers tap into our  cane energies and a bunch of other stuff  one dude Willem the head of researcher  at Bergen worth figure this was probably  a bad plan but his protege Lawrence  decided to roll with it and founded the  Church has a way to distribute the blood  ministrations far and wide believing  that it would evolve mankind to a higher  level of being publicly the healing  church uses the ministrations to perform  miracles but privately the choir is  using them as a way to strengthen their  contact with the old ones under the  guidance of a being named Everett us  either way blood ministrations are a hit  and yharnam grows into a sprawling  Dickensian metropolis drawing people in  from near and far  however willem was right in the whole  process winds up turning random people  into monsters every cosmic event or so  the people of Jarno however with their  entire society revolving around the  healing church in these blood  administrations considers this to be an  acceptable side effect and a couple  dudes in the church's workshop ludwig in  gehrman came up with a simple solution  just to hunt down anyone who turns into  a werewolf and kill them thus the hunt  was born in the present you play as a  character who has come to yharnam to get  blood ministration to cure some  unspecified problem and in exchange for  the Cure you are roped into joining the  hunt over the course of the game you  discover that the hunt is basically a  covert way of maintaining the status quo  because every time a great one tries to  reproduce that's what triggers everyone  turning into monsters so the job of the  hunters is to kill monster people yes  but the main goal is to kill the new  baby old one in order to prolong  humanity's existence just a little bit  longer it's even more intricate than  that the lore notes scattered around the  world pepper and dozens of different  individuals and a handful of various  factions each with their own goals and  complex relationships to one another and  this is also where things start to go to  the weird side see like with a lot of  cosmic horror much of the logic of  blood-borne is founded on the idea that  people are simply incapable of seeing  things that are too alien that a giant  eldritch abomination could be hanging  out in the middle of town and even  though it would be right in front of you  you have no frame of reference to  comprehend it so your brain would simply  not know how to process it and would  just blank it out by extension the more  you know about the truth of the cosmos  the more you're able to see because you  now have a frame to put it in the  downside is that this shift in  perspective leads to madness or at the  very least a disconnection with the  ordinary in such a way that most would  interpret as madness with this in mind a  large portion of the hunt  place in this kind of altered state of  perception with the hunter seem fighting  and interacting with things that aren't  normally visible and that brings us to  the endings of the game after the player  survives the night and completes the  hunt they return to the hunter's dream a  pocket dimension that serves as the game  central hub and are told to go talk with  gehrman now gehrman's been sitting  around for the entire game as a kindly  old if slightly perverted mentor figure  you're welcome to use whatever you find  even the doll should be pleased but if  the player has been paying attention and  snooping around then they've pieced  together that the dude should have to be  pushing like 150 200 years by now he was  an original hunter so something's up  you go to talk to gehrman he  congratulate you on a job well done and  this is where the game splits into its  three endings the game offers you a  choice to submit or to resist if you  submit to gehrman he executes you in the  dream and you wake up the following  morning back in the real world to a new  sunrise on a new day if you resist then  you're forced to fight gehrman after his  defeat an entity approaches you picks  you up and you black out waking up in  gehrman's wheelchair as the new gehrman  the new keeper of the dream the new  director of the hunt however if you jump  through a bunch of Hoops throughout the  game collecting pieces of the old ones  and consuming them then you resist the  moon presence fight it and wake up as a  squid creature you have become an infant  old one yourself okay so if the three  endings are wake up as a squid wake up  as a slave and wake up to a new sunrise  how is the happiest ending actually the  worst ending well it's because of  knowledge and understanding the sunrise  ending is tragic because of what it  costs as gehrman tells you before your  execution you wake up having forgotten  everything about the hunt everything you  have learned about the old ones the  healing church the choir the truth about  yharnam and bergen work the fact that  humanity's most powerful institutions  are being steered by cosmic forces that  have their own unintelligible agendas  and conflicts that routinely wreak havoc  and destruction on  lady that the one institution that seems  to fight for the protection of humanity  the hunters is itself the construct of  some cosmic being that humanity is just  a pawn in an alien Civil War your hunter  gets to go on to live a long happy life  in a doomed world this is so  thematically poignant that this comes at  the end of a Souls born game  gameplay-wise the player has spent hours  and hours up to this point being taught  through experience how much it sucks to  get taken out by some unseen unstoppable  thing most of the players first  half-hour in the nightmare of mensis the  final major chunk of the game is going  to be spent yelling what's even killing  me as the player is bombarded by  insanity rays from this light house and  that's the tragedy of the happy ending  you're doomed  but you don't even know how dumb you are  in order to go back to a normal life all  that insight needs to be taken away or  else you would still see the things that  lurk around the corners of reality which  would ultimately drive you mad or to  something others would interpret as  madness the only way to be happy in the  world of blood-borne is to be ignorant  of reality conversely the best ending is  itself bittersweet with the hunter  gaining incomprehensible power being  evolved beyond the limitations of the  human form but at the cost of the last  remaining vestiges of their humanity as  with all souls born games there's an  element of futility in each ending a  cycle that the player is ultimately  incapable of breaking away from if the  player accepts gehrman's offer the cycle  of the hunt continues because the hunter  has forgotten everything about its true  nature  if the player rejects gehrman's offer  the hunter simply replaces him and the  hunt continues with the hunter compelled  to participate a slave to the cycle if  the player rejects the offering defeats  the moon presence then the hunter  transcends into a thing that has no  reason to care about humanity or the  fate of humans the exact outcome of this  scenario is more ambiguous than the  others but chances are it doesn't turn  out smashingly for the common folk of  the world and this is part of what makes  blood-borne such a good example of its  genre  it's a faithfulness to the kinds of  existential dread the sense of futility  that makes cosmic horror not just scary  but haunting because the longer you  think about it the more you accept that  you should indeed  fear the old blood

